From a04648641ff2d0ad2602f84fa04aa28029afd85f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andrey Borodin <amborodin@acm.org>
Date: Thu, 6 Aug 2026 12:03:11 +0500
Subject: [PATCH v1] Avoid streaming zero-filled WAL switch padding

Forced WAL switches can leave nearly a whole segment as zero padding.
Sending those bytes wastes network bandwidth and can delay synchronous
replication, particularly on low-traffic servers that use archive_timeout.

Add a physical replication message that represents zero-filled
end-of-segment padding.  Walsender emits one such message after detecting a
zero WAL page.  The server walreceiver and uncompressed frontend WAL writers
reconstruct the tail with truncate and extend, allowing the filesystem to
store it sparsely.  Compressed frontend writers generate the zeros locally.

Add a recovery test that verifies the reconstructed segment byte for byte
and checks that its tail is sparse.
